{
  "dom_id": "step-q341218",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Let $F(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n\nII. $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n\nIII. $f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$",
  "worked_solution": "Since $f$ is the $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$-periodic extension of $F,$ we have  \n\n- $f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\pi /3$-periodicity:\n\n$$\nf(x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, take $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=f(0+\\frac{\\pi }{3})=f(0)=F(0)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)\\ne F(x)$ for $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, take $x=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})=f(0+\\frac{\\pi }{6})=f(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=F(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=f(0)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\ne f(x)$ for $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\".",
